        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: plōrō, plōrāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "plōrō, plōrāre — \"to wail, cry aloud\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puella plōrat, quia pila eius in flūmen cecidit. The girl is wailing because her ball fell into the river."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "plōrō, plōrāre, plōrāvī, plōrātum. Louder than fleō: crying out loud. English deplore and implore come from its compounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "plōrō is a native Latin verb whose origin is not settled; Lewis & Short calls it doubtful. English deplore and implore come from its compounds dēplōrō and implōrō."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
